  {
    "@id": "https://www.osac.ca/genid/4fd0127ab4feb9b8",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/Article"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.osac.ca/visual-media-arts/event/112-VA4280/a-selection-of-specimens-battlefords-allied-arts-council&Itemid=1819"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/inLanguage": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "en-GB"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/articleBody": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\n        A Selection of Specimens\nWed, Jul 1, 2026 to Sun, Aug 23, 2026 @ Chapel Gallery\nCommunity: Battlefords' Allied Arts Council\n\n Education Package\n Condition Report\n List of Works\n Care & Handling\n Press Release\n\n  \n  Kristin Teetaert, \"plantabis \"dentes et supercilium\" \", hand-dyed wool, clay, (and aluminum wire), dimensions variable, 2023\n\nA Selection of Specimens is a solo exhibition be artist Kristin Teetaert. This exhibition features drawings, felt sculptures, and painted tiles. Kristin states: \"The specimens came to life in 2012 in the form of a charcoal gestural sketch. The idea of sculpting them in wool followed. Wool allowed me to use bright colours and to create sculptures that were malleable. A tactile person, I wanted to play with my sculptures. This idea of being able to play with and manipulate the sculptures inspired the tiles; they are all able to interconnect with each other on all four sides. An important aspect of them was the ability to create different paths between the specimens by physically moving the tiles, or as a viewer, by following the shapes as they weave through the installation. I often alternate between two and three dimensions as I work. \n\nThey are surprisingly autobiographical.  I have always had a fascination with botany and nature, and a love of the unique shapes found in the natural and microscopic world. This, combined with my love of colour, has resulted in this series of playful sculptures and drawings that explore the ideas of interconnection, growth, spontaneity, and evolution.\"A Selection of Specimens is a solo exhibition be artist Kristin Teetaert. This exhibition features drawings, felt sculptures, and painted tiles. Kristin states: \"The specimens came to life in 2012 in the form of a charcoal gestural sketch. The idea of sculpting them in wool followed. Wool allowed me to use bright colours and to create sculptures that were malleable. A tactile person, I wanted to play with my sculptures. This idea of being able to play with and manipulate the sculptures inspired the tiles; they are all able to interconnect with each other on all four sides. An important aspect of them was the ability to create different paths between the specimens by physically moving the tiles, or as a viewer, by following the shapes as they weave through the installation. I often alternate between two and three dimensions as I work. \n\nThey are surprisingly autobiographical.  I have always had a fascination with botany and nature, and a love of the unique shapes found in the natural and microscopic world. This, combined with my love of colour, has resulted in this series of playful sculptures and drawings that explore the ideas of interconnection, growth, spontaneity, and evolution.\"\nOther Exhibitions:\n\nWed, Jul 1, 2026 to Sun, Aug 23, 2026 at Chapel Gallery\nTue, Sep 1, 2026 to Wed, Sep 23, 2026 at Central Park Library/Arts Centre\nThu, Oct 1, 2026 to Mon, Nov 23, 2026 at Community pARTners Gallery\nTue, Dec 1, 2026 to Sat, Jan 23, 2027 at John V. Hicks Gallery at the Margo Fournier Arts Centre\nSat, May 1, 2027 to Wed, Jun 23, 2027 at Humboldt and District Gallery\n\n    "
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/headline": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\n            Exhibition        "
      }
    ]
  },
